[Music]  i started thinking about this film  originally in 2012  after a story that i had read about a  woman  named savita jalapenova who died in  ireland  after being denied a life-saving  abortion and i  became really obsessed with reading  about what is insensitively coined  abortion tourism  these long you know distressing  journeys that people take all over the  world  to access very essential  abortion care and i think i'm  really compelled to tell stories  about kind of painful journeys that  people  go through that you haven't seen on  screen  i found an article about new york that  was all about how new york is the  abortion capital  of the united states and it's a place  where you can get  the care that you need and  i i said it in pennsylvania because  pennsylvania is one of 37 states that  has parental consent laws that require  teenagers to ask their parents or go  see a judge if they need an abortion  i was exploring the film through the  point of view of  a teenager i felt like it was realistic  that she could take this trip  from pennsylvania to new york i was just  going about my everyday life working in  a grocery store and  playing a band and living with like four  roommates  and um just one day i get an email  you know asking me to try out for this  part and it was  just super bizarre and um came as a  total surprise to me  and at first i immediately declined  immediately it was just thought to  myself i'm not an actor  and um i just thought it was something i  wasn't capable of  and it was also like a terrifying  opportunity because  of the fact that it was something so new  and so big i chatted with scott eliza's  partner a bit  and you know he told me like well you're  a performer and you know  you can totally do it and um he  convinced me to you know like  skype with eliza and i talked with her  and i found her really easy to get along  with  and i liked her ideas and um  and then i read the script and you know  i was then i became further invested  into the story  i really loved just like the just like  how grounded all the characters felt  and how real it felt and like i just had  a feeling that it was going to be a very  powerful story  and that it was just going to be the  type of  project that would be really humbling to  work on  i met with kelly chapman who worked at  choices clinic  in queens and together  we began to try and figure out like a  way to marry what she does as a social  worker  how she behaves in the office  with the style of the script she was a  real  sounding board for me while i was  writing the scripts  i kept on calling her and asking her  questions and while i was writing the  script  i began to sort of hear her voice and  the character on the page  and i knew that it was important to cast  her in the end  um because i had essentially  accidentally like  written the role for her and i felt that  it would  create um a really safe space for  sydney a first time actor to perform the  scene  there's something about this like  vulnerable first-time actor  being you know in the care of a real  social worker  that allowed for a certain level  of emotional vulnerability i  wanted the pivotal scene of the film and  never rarely  sometimes always to be truly  authentic it was important to me  to consult with planned parenthood and  other clinics while writing the script  and i sat with a lot of social workers  and began to try and understand  as a writer what their job is and  how they would engage with somebody who  was a minor  traveling from out of state they took me  through the process  the questionnaire form and that's where  i found the intimate partner violence  section  in which a social worker would try and  assess whether or not  the person who was in their office was  safe or not  i was working at an abortion clinic in  queens when eliza was doing  research she wanted specifically  to talk about not just my experience  as a counselor an abortion counselor but  specifically you know wanted to hear  some stories  about women who women and girls who had  traveled  and of course there were a lot of  stories it was a really important part  of the story for me  because autumn as a character is  traveling through  such an unsafe landscape  you know there's no place in her world  that's completely safe  until she arrives in the office of the  social worker  hi autumn kelly i'm your counselor how  are you doing  okay  i want to spend a few minutes talking  with you about your relationships okay  because they can affect your health did  you know that  no all right so i'm going to ask you  some questions  they can be really personal and all you  have to do is answer either never  rarely sometimes or always it's kind of  like multiple choice but it's not a test  okay okay in the past year  your partner has refused to wear a  condom  never rarely sometimes  always  sometimes okay and your partner messes  with your birth control or tries to get  you pregnant when you don't want to be  never rarely  sometimes always  never okay your partner's threatened or  frightened you  never rarely  sometimes always  why are you asking me this i want to  make sure that you're safe  your partner's threatened or frightened  you  never rarely  sometimes always  oh rarely okay  your partner has hit you slapped you or  physically hurt you  never rarely  sometimes always  has your partner ever hit you slapped  you or physically hurt you  is someone hurting you  it's okay  it's just a couple more questions all  right  your partners made you have sex when you  didn't want to  never rarely  sometimes always  it's okay  i want to make sure you're safe and i  want to help you if i can  i have just one more question for you  okay autumn  has anyone forced you into a sexual act  ever in your lifetime yes or no  uh yeah okay  do you wanna tell me about it no no it's  okay  i'm gonna give you my number and you can  call me  we don't have to talk about it today but  you can call me if you need to talk or  if you need some help okay  do you have any questions for me um  is it the procedure is it gonna be  painful  it's gonna be a little uncomfortable  have you ever had a gyn exam before  no okay it's going to be in an  operating room there's going to be a  doctor there's going to be a nurse  there's going to be a medical assistant  i can be there with you if you want me  to be  would you like me to go with you sure  okay  okay so it's kind of like having a gyn  exam today  they're going to put a speculum in the  vagina  they're going to put laminaria into the  cervix and that's what's going to help  the cervix open overnight  all right so it's going to feel  uncomfortable kind of like pressure kind  of like you have to pee but it shouldn't  hurt  okay okay if you have any really bad  pain tonight you have to call us  okay then when you come back tomorrow  for the second part of the procedure for  that part you're gonna be asleep  okay and i can be with you then too i'm  gonna like go under  okay just for about 10 minutes it's  pretty quick all right  and then when you wake up you'll be in  the recovery room you're gonna be all  done  and you could go home okay all right  are you ready i think so  okay when it came to preparing for this  scene  i spent a lot of time reflecting  on my own memories and experiences  as a young woman which can be difficult  and sometimes rather hard to think about  and  when i thought about this scene i  thought you know this is somebody who's  finally making herself vulnerable  and feel safe enough to do so and you  know so tapping into my own  places and experiences and getting into  that headspace  was important and working with  the social worker who was a real social  worker made me feel comfortable enough  to  dive into that place and to really  let my own experiences inform the scene  basically i did a counseling session i  did a counseling session like i've done  i think i've seen like somewhere in the  neighborhood of like 15  to 20 000 women in like the decade that  i was  that i was doing abortion counseling so  i've had this conversation  many many many times so i just had to  have the conversation i just had to put  myself in that headspace and  do what i do there was  the piece of the script  where she didn't want me to go off  script  at all um and that's  you know specifically when we get to the  likert scale  there were specific responses that she  wanted me  to give the day that we shot the scene  i made sure that sydney had a  space to sit away from the commotion  of the production a quiet office  and she sat in there for a long time and  she read the scene  several times and i remember right  before we shot i came  in and i told her i said don't worry  about  the character's medical history you can  just  answer as yourself because the beginning  of the scene  just begins with a lot of medical  information medical history  and there was something about sydney  starting from a very personal place  speaking about her own life her own  history  that allowed for her to dig much deeper  in the more sort of emotional and  heightened  section of the scene i chose to shoot  the scene  very simply you know i wanted the camera  to be very still  in front of her and static and  i wanted it almost to be  like a mirror for the character and i  wanted  it to feel a little like an  interrogation and it was important for  me that the audience  sit through the entire scene like in a  long take  and for it to play out in a durational  sense  because i wanted them to experience what  it's like to really sit in one of those  offices  and particularly i wanted to bring a  male audience into such an intimate  experience  to film the scene for me it was  really powerful it felt  [Music]  amazing to be able to harness my own  experiences and emotions  from maybe my past or even just what i  was dealing with in the present  and you know considering what autumn had  gone through  and harnessing all that to create a  moment  that so many people have connected with  and um  were moved by and i remember i believe i  said that i felt  cathartic afterwards and it felt  just like it just felt like so much  energy had left my body  and i felt um just so  alive eliza said you know i just  as we're going through the casting  process i just keep hearing your voice  in my head  and i would really love for you to do it  and my my initial reaction was like  oh my god i don't know how to do this  like are there gonna be like cue cards  or something like i have  no idea about how any of this works  but she said no i i just want you to  i want you to be yourself i want you to  do  what you've done a million times before  and just have the conversation that  you've had so many times before so  she wasn't asking me to be anyone other  than who i  am so i completely  trusted her i completely trusted eliza  um you know i don't know that i would  have  said yes and been involved in that  capacity  with just anyone but i i knew i knew  from  my you know previous conversations and  interactions with her that  this was gonna be a project that i would  be  very happy to have my my name and my  professional reputation attached to um  i remember in the moment shooting that  scene that  it felt very powerful to me personally  just the experience of doing it shooting  it was  it was intense you know i had hopes that  other people would think the same  and you know seeing now that they have  it's um  really validating and it's uh it feels  really good  i wanted the audience to understand  the depth of her trauma  and how much she is how much pain she's  carrying around  with her and how the situation of having  to travel  and to do it all on her own like how  much  trauma that can add for a real person  in this country who's navigating similar  [Music]  situations

